<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Karamanids</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1487 CE (approx.) · capital Karaman</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful Turkish beylik of Anatolia after the Ottomans, centred on Karaman and Konya and their chief rival for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Karamanids took their name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman-bey">Karaman Bey</a>, son of Nure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> leader in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> mountains. Around 1250 they rose against the weakened <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamanoglu-mehmed-bey">Mehmed Bey</a> briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1277, where he reportedly decreed that Turkish alone be used in government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Karamanids held the old Seljuk heartland, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Larende</a> (Karaman) and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, and saw themselves as heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. They allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-bey">Alaeddin Bey</a> was defeated and executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> in 1397, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored the dynasty in 1402. They built mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aktekke-mosque">Ak Tekke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuniye-madrasa">Hatuniye</a> in Karaman.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in 1468, and the last Karamanid claimants were defeated by the 1480s; the region became the Ottoman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karamanids.jpg" alt="Karamanids" width="320" height="304"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Mediterranean_1450.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1487 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karaman</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karamanoglu Mehmed Bey, Alaeddin Bey, Ibrahim II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karamanid Beylik, Karamanoğulları, Beylik of Karaman</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1368&amp;focus=karamanids" aria-label="Karamanids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5512 839 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karamanids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5832 1095L5810 1072M5832 1095L6582 1190M5832 1095L5709 970M5832 1095L5815 1335M5832 1095L5804 1116"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5810 1072h0M6582 1190h0M5709 970h0M5815 1335h0M5804 1116h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5832 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5832 1095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Karaman. <a href="/atlas?year=1368&amp;focus=karamanids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
